  "body": [
    "How does the Playbook Adobe integration work?",
    "Which Adobe apps does Playbook work with?",
    "How does Playbook stay the source of truth?",
    "How production and design teams use it",
    "Which plans include Adobe sync?",
    "Frequently asked questions",
    "Playbook's Adobe DAM integration runs through the desktop app's two-way sync. Connect a board to a folder on your machine, open a file in Photoshop, Illustrator, or InDesign, and edit it natively. When you save, the change syncs back to your library on its own. No File then Export, no re-upload, no naming anything hero_v4_FINAL. Premiere Pro project files and renders live in the same synced folder, so video work stays organized alongside everything else. A green checkmark confirms the current version is in the workspace, and every save is kept in the asset's version history. Adobe does the editing. Playbook stays the single source of truth.",
    "Most digital asset management for Adobe still works one direction at a time. You drag an asset in, edit it, then manually export and re-upload the result as a new file. Every hand-off is a round trip, and every round trip is a chance to lose the thread between the version on disk and the version your team can see. Playbook closes that loop: edit locally, and the current version is already in the workspace for reviewers, clients, and teammates.",
    "Want to see it against your own files? Schedule a demo.",
    "The Playbook Adobe integration is a folder-based two-way sync, not a plugin you check files out of. It lives in the Playbook Desktop App, which mirrors your chosen boards to a folder on disk and pushes local changes back automatically. Three steps cover it:",
    "Sync a board to a folder. Install the desktop app, sign in, and pick which boards to sync. Playbook mirrors them to disk, and a green checkmark on each file confirms it matches the workspace. Synced files also work offline.",
    "Open and edit in your Adobe app. Double-click a .psd, .ai, or .indd file and it opens in Photoshop, Illustrator, or InDesign like any local file, with full native performance.",
    "Save, and the edit syncs back. As Playbook's tutorial puts it, changes sync so \"your team sees the latest version, no exporting, re-uploading, or version-naming required.\" The new version joins the asset's history, and the board updates for everyone who can see it.",
    "The whole difference in one line: the plugin model asks you to export out, Playbook asks you to save.",
    "Playbook's two-way sync is built around Photoshop, Illustrator, and InDesign, the apps whose edited files live on disk as .psd, .ai, and .indd. Open one from your sync folder, edit, save, and the change flows back. Because the integration is folder-based, it also carries the outputs those apps produce, so exported JPEGs, PNGs, and PDFs sync the same way.",
    "Premiere Pro fits through the same folder. Your Premiere project files and exported renders live in the synced library alongside your stills and design files, so cuts get reviewed, versioned, and shared in one place. The honest boundary: Playbook is not a timeline editor, so you cut in Premiere, and Playbook is where the output gets organized and approved.",
    "Timing makes this useful. In October 2024, Adobe discontinued Creative Cloud synced files for business and enterprise plans, and teams that relied on a folder syncing in the background lost exactly that. Playbook gives the behavior back, and the same folder is a full library with search, comments, and approvals around it. Stated plainly: Playbook is not an image or video editor. Adobe does the editing. Playbook is the asset library that syncs with it.",
    "Playbook keeps the library, the version history, and the sync status in one system that the folder on disk reflects. The green checkmark is the honest signal that a file matches the workspace, so you never wonder whether the version a client sees is the one you last saved. Version history is on every plan: each save-back snapshots the prior state into the asset's Revisions panel, where you can compare and revert non-destructively (the revert window is 7 days on Free and Pro, unlimited on Team and up, at the time of writing). Synced files stay available offline, and on Team and up, custom sync folders map boards to your own folder structure instead of a single default.",
    "Teams that live in Adobe use Playbook as the library under their editing tools that keeps everyone on the current version. WBD Sports runs broadcast marketing across TNT Sports, Eurosport, and Bleacher Report with more than 10,000 assets in Playbook. After moving off scattered SharePoint and Google Drive folders, the team cut review from four rounds to two and dropped asset requests by roughly 50%. As David Bret, VP of Marketing for WBD Sports EMEA, put it: \"It's a self-service central repository. Fewer bottlenecks, fewer mixups, and more autonomy.\"",
    "Invictus Media, a production company where \"90% of the work we do involves some sort of video,\" keeps more than 33,000 assets in Playbook after migrating off a 5TB Dropbox plan. For a shop producing in Adobe tools all day, the same fit applies to any creative agency or media and entertainment team juggling client work across a stack of Adobe files.",
    "Playbook Sync, the desktop app's two-way syncing, is available on Pro and above, and custom sync folders start on Team (at the time of writing). Pricing is published, not quote-gated, so you can confirm current tiers on the pricing page. Guests stay free on paid plans, so reviewers and clients never take a seat to comment on your Adobe work.",
    "No. You edit the file in your Adobe app and save it normally. The Playbook Desktop App detects the change and syncs it back automatically, with no File then Export step and no re-upload. A green checkmark confirms the current version is in the library.",
    "Two-way sync is built around Photoshop, Illustrator, and InDesign, where edited files live on disk. Because it is folder-based, it also carries the exports those apps produce, plus Premiere Pro project files and renders. Playbook is the library and review layer, not a timeline editor.",
    "Every plan keeps version history. Each save-back snapshots the previous state into the asset's Revisions panel, where you can compare and revert. The revert window is 7 days on Free and Pro, and unlimited on Team and up, at the time of writing.",
    "Stop exporting and re-uploading your Adobe files. Connect a board, edit in Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, or InDesign, and let the library keep itself current. Schedule a demo to see the two-way sync against your own workflow."
  ],
